#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:1041
#, no-c-format
msgid "Configuring your system to use &kdm;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1043
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This chapter assumes that your system is already configured to run the &X-"
"Window;, and that you only need to reconfigure it to allow graphical login."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:1048
#, no-c-format
msgid "Setting up &kdm;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1050
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The fundamental thing that controls whether your computer boots to a "
"terminal prompt (console mode) or a graphical login prompt is the default "
"runlevel. The runlevel is set by the program <application> <ulink url=\"man:"
"init\">/sbin/init</ulink></application> under the control of the "
"configuration file <filename>/etc/inittab</filename>. The default runlevels "
"used by different &UNIX; systems (and different &Linux; distributions) vary, "
"but if you look at <filename>/etc/inittab</filename> the start of it should "
"be something like this:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screen
#: index.docbook:1059
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"# Default runlevel. The runlevels used by RHS are:\n"
"# 0 - halt (Do NOT set initdefault to this)\n"
"# 1 - Single user mode\n"
"# 2 - Multiuser, without NFS\n"
"# 3 - Full multiuser mode\n"
"# 4 - unused\n"
"# 5 - X11\n"
"# 6 - reboot (Do NOT set initdefault to this)\n"
"\n"
"id:3:initdefault:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1061
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"All but the last line of this extract are comments. The comments show that "
"runlevel 5 is used for X11 and that runlevel 3 is used for multi-user mode "
"without X11 (console mode). The final line specifies that the default "
"runlevel of the system is 3 (console mode). If your system currently uses "
"graphical login (for example, using &xdm;) its default runlevel will match "
"the runlevel specified for X11."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1068
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The runlevel with graphical login (&xdm;) for some common &Linux; "
"distributions is:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1072
#, no-c-format
msgid "5 for &RedHat; 3.x and later, and for &Mandrake;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1073
#, no-c-format
msgid "4 for Slackware"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1074
#, no-c-format
msgid "3 for &SuSE;. 4.x and 5.x"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1077
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The first step in configuring your system is to ensure that you can start "
"&kdm; from the command line. Once this is working, you can change your "
"system configuration so that &kdm; starts automatically each time you reboot "
"your system."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1082
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To test &kdm;, you must first bring your system to a runlevel that does not "
"run &xdm;. To do so, issue a command like this:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screen
#: index.docbook:1085
#, fuzzy, no-c-format
#| msgid "/sbin/init <option>3</option>"
msgid "<command>/sbin/init <option>3</option></command>"
msgstr "/sbin/init <option>3</option>"

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1087
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Instead of the number <option>3</option> you should specify the appropriate "
"runlevel for console mode on your system."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1090
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If your system uses Pluggable Authentication Modules (<abbrev>PAM</abbrev>), "
"which is normal with recent &Linux; and &Solaris; systems, you should check "
"that your <abbrev>PAM</abbrev> configuration permits login through the "
"service named <literal>kde</literal>. If you previously used &xdm; "
"successfully, you should not need to make any changes to your <abbrev>PAM</"
"abbrev> configuration in order to use &kdm;. <filename>/etc/pam.conf</"
"filename> or <filename>/etc/pam.d/kde</filename>. Information on configuring "
"<abbrev>PAM</abbrev> is beyond the scope of this handbook, but <abbrev>PAM</"
"abbrev> comes with comprehensive documentation (try looking in <filename>/"
"usr/share/doc/*pam*/html/</filename>)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1102
#, no-c-format
msgid "Now it's time for you to test &kdm; by issuing the following command:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screen
#: index.docbook:1105
#, no-c-format
msgid "<command>kdm <option>-nodaemon</option></command>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1107
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you get a &kdm; login dialog and you are able to log in, things are going "
"well. The main thing that can go wrong here is that the run-time linker "
"might not find the shared &Qt; or &kde; libraries. If you have a binary "
"distribution of the &kde; libraries, make sure &kdm; is installed where the "
"libraries believe &kde; is installed and try setting some environment "
"variables to point to your &kde; and &Qt; libraries."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1115
#, no-c-format
msgid "For example:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screen
#: index.docbook:1117
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<command>export \n"
"<option>KDEDIR=<replaceable>/opt/kde</replaceable></option></command>\n"
"<command>export \n"
"<option>QTDIR=<replaceable>/usr/lib/qt4</replaceable></option></command>\n"
"<command>export \n"
"<option>PATH=<replaceable>$KDEDIR/bin:$QTDIR/bin:$PATH</replaceable></"
"option></command>\n"
"<command>export \n"
"<option>LD_LIBRARY_PATH=<replaceable>$KDEDIR/lib:$QTDIR/lib</replaceable></"
"option></command>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1119
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you are still unsuccessful, try starting &xdm; instead, to make sure that "
"you are not suffering from a more serious X configuration problem."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1123
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"When you are able to start &kdm; successfully, you can start to replace "
"&xdm; by &kdm;. Again, this is distribution-dependent."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1128
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"For &RedHat;, edit <filename>/etc/inittab</filename>, look for this line:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screen
#: index.docbook:1130
#, no-c-format
msgid "x:5:respawn:/usr/X11/bin/xdm -nodaemon"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1131
#, no-c-format
msgid "and replace with:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screen
#: index.docbook:1132
#, no-c-format
msgid "x:5:respawn:/opt/kde/bin/kdm"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1133
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This tells <command>init</command>(8) to respawn &kdm; when the system is in "
"run level 5. Note that &kdm; does not need the <option>-nodaemon</option> "
"option."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1138
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"For &Mandrake;, the X11 runlevel in <filename>/etc/inittab</filename> "
"invokes the shell script <filename>/etc/X11/prefdm</filename>, which is set "
"up to select from amongst several display managers, including &kdm;. Make "
"sure that all the paths are correct for your installation."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1145
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"For &SuSE;, edit <filename>/sbin/init.d/xdm</filename> to add a first line:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screen
#: index.docbook:1148
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
". /etc/rc.config\n"
"DISPLAYMANAGER=kdm\n"
"export DISPLAYMANAGER"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1150
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"For FreeBSD, edit <filename>/etc/ttys</filename> and find the line like this:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screen
#: index.docbook:1152
#, no-c-format
msgid "ttyv8   \"/usr/X11R6/bin/xdm -nodaemon\"  xterm   off secure"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1153
#, no-c-format
msgid "and edit it to this:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screen
#: index.docbook:1154
#, no-c-format
msgid "ttyv8   \"/opt/kde/bin/kdm\"  xterm   on secure"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1157
#, no-c-format
msgid "Most other distributions are a variation of one of these."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1161
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"At this stage, you can test &kdm; again by bringing your system to the "
"runlevel that should now run &kdm;. To do so, issue a command like this:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screen
#: index.docbook:1165
#, fuzzy, no-c-format
#| msgid "/sbin/init <option>3</option>"
msgid "<command>/sbin/init <option>5</option></command>"
msgstr "/sbin/init <option>3</option>"

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1167
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Instead of the number <option>5</option> you should specify the appropriate "
"runlevel for running X11 on your system."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1170
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The final step is to edit the <parameter>initdefault</parameter> entry in "
"<filename>/etc/inittab</filename> to specify the appropriate runlevel for "
"X11."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1174
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Before you make this change, ensure that you have a way to reboot your "
"system if a problem occurs. This might be a <quote>rescue</quote> floppy-"
"disk provided by your operating system distribution or a specially-designed "
"<quote>rescue</quote> floppy-disk, such as <literal>tomsrtbt</literal>. "
"Ignore this advice at your peril."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1181
#, no-c-format
msgid "This usually involves changing the line:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screen
#: index.docbook:1182
#, no-c-format
msgid "id:3:initdefault:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1183
#, no-c-format
msgid "<para>to</para>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screen
#: index.docbook:1184
#, no-c-format
msgid "id:5:initdefault:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1186
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"When you reboot your system, you should end up with the graphical &kdm; "
"login dialog."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1189
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If this step is unsuccessful the most likely problem is that the environment "
"used at boot time differs from the environment that you used for testing at "
"the command line. If you are trying to get two versions of &kde; to co-"
"exist, be particularly careful that the settings you use for your "
"<envar>PATH</envar> and <envar>LD_LIBRARY_PATH</envar> environment variables "
"are consistent, and that the startup scripts are not over-riding them in "
"some way."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:1202
#, no-c-format
msgid "Supporting multiple window managers"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1204
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&kdm; detects most available window managers and desktop environments when "
"it is run. Installing a new one should make it automatically available in "
"the &kdm; main dialog's <guimenuitem>Session Type</guimenuitem> submenu."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1208
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you have a very new window manager, or something that &kdm; does not "
"support, the first thing you should check is that the executable to be run "
"is in the <envar>PATH</envar> and has not been renamed by the distributor "
"into something unexpected."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1213
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If the case is that the session type is not supported by &kdm; yet (maybe "
"because it is too new), you can quite easily add it."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1216
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The session types are defined by <firstterm>.desktop</firstterm> files "
"located in the directories listed in <link linkend=\"option-sessionsdirs"
"\"><option>SessionsDirs</option></link>. The last named directory contains "
"the system-provided default session types and is <filename class=\"directory"
"\">$<envar>KDEDIR</envar>/share/apps/kdm/sessions</filename> in an "
"installation from source. Software upgrades will typically overwrite "
"anything in here, so it is unwise to use it for configuration purposes. "
"Instead, a separate configuration directory should be listed first. It is "
"set to <filename class=\"directory\">$<envar>KDEDIR</envar>/share/config/kdm/"
"sessions</filename> in an installation from source, but distributors often "
"change it to something like <filename class=\"directory\">/etc/kde4/kdm/"
"sessions</filename>. You can simply add an appropriately named <literal role="
"\"extension\">.desktop</literal> files here. The fields are:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: index.docbook:1232
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"[Desktop Entry]\n"
"Encoding=UTF-8 <lineannotation>This is fixed to <option>UTF-8</option> and\n"
"may be omitted</lineannotation>\n"
"Type=XSession <lineannotation>This is fixed to <option>XSession</option> "
"and\n"
"may be omitted</lineannotation>\n"
"Exec=<replaceable>executable name</replaceable> <lineannotation>Passed to\n"
"<command>eval exec</command> in a Bourne shell</lineannotation>\n"
"TryExec=<replaceable>executable name</replaceable> "
"<lineannotation>Supported\n"
"but not required</lineannotation>\n"
"Name=<replaceable>name to show in the &kdm; session list</replaceable>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1234
#, no-c-format
msgid "There are also three <quote>magic</quote> types:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: term
#: index.docbook:1238
#, no-c-format
msgid "default"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1240
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The default session for &kdm; is normally &kde; but can be configured by the "
"system administrator."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: term
#: index.docbook:1247
#, no-c-format
msgid "custom"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1249
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The Custom session will run the user's ~/.xsession if it exists, falling "
"back to the default session otherwise."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: term
#: index.docbook:1256
#, no-c-format
msgid "failsafe"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1258
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Failsafe will run a very plain session, and is useful only for debugging "
"purposes."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1266
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To override a session type, copy the .desktop file from the data dir to the "
"config dir and edit it at will. Removing the shipped session types can be "
"accomplished by <quote>shadowing</quote> them with .desktop files containing "
"Hidden=true. For the magic session types no .desktop files exist by default, "
"but &kdm; pretends they would, so you can override them like any other type."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:1276
#, no-c-format
msgid "Using &kdm; for Remote Logins (&XDMCP;)"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1278
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&XDMCP; is the Open Group standard, the <quote>X Display Manager Control "
"Protocol</quote>. This is used to set up connections between remote systems "
"over the network."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1282
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&XDMCP; is useful in multiuser situations where there are users with "
"workstations and a more powerful server that can provide the resources to "
"run multiple X sessions. For example, &XDMCP; is a good way to reuse old "
"computers - a Pentium or even 486 computer with 16 Mb RAM is sufficient to "
"run X itself, and using &XDMCP; such a computer can run a full modern &kde; "
"session from a server. For the server part, once a single &kde; (or other "
"environment) session is running, running another one requires very few extra "
"resources."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1291
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"However, allowing another method of login to your machine obviously has "
"security implications. You should run this service only if you need to allow "
"remote X Servers to start login sessions on your system. Users with a single "
"&UNIX; computer should not need to run this."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:1300
#, no-c-format
msgid "Advanced Topics"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:1303
#, fuzzy, no-c-format
#| msgid "Commands"
msgid "Command Sockets"
msgstr "ពាក្យ​បញ្ជា"

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1305
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This is a feature you can use to remote-control &kdm;. It's mostly intended "
"for use by &ksmserver; and &kdesktop; from a running session, but other "
"applications are possible as well."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1309
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The sockets are &UNIX; domain sockets which live in subdirectories of the "
"directory specified by <option>FifoDir</option>=. The subdir is the key to "
"addressing and security; the sockets all have the file name "
"<filename>socket</filename> and file permissions <literal>rw-rw-rw-</"
"literal> (0666). This is because some systems don't care for the file "
"permission of the socket files."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1316
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"There are two types of sockets: the global one (dmctl) and the per-display "
"ones (dmctl-&lt;display&gt;)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1319
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The global one's subdir is owned by root, the subdirs of the per-display "
"ones' are owned by the user currently owning the session (root or the logged "
"in user). Group ownership of the subdirs can be set via FifoGroup=, "
"otherwise it's root. The file permissions of the subdirs are rwxr-x--- "
"(0750)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1325
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The fields of a command are separated by tabs (<token>\\t</token>), the "
"fields of a list are separated by spaces, literal spaces in list fields are "
"denoted by <token>\\s</token>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1329
#, no-c-format
msgid "The command is terminated by a newline (<token>\\n</token>)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1331
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The same applies to replies. The reply on success is <returnvalue>ok</"
"returnvalue>, possibly followed by the requested information. The reply on "
"error is an errno-style word (&eg; <returnvalue>perm</returnvalue>, "
"<returnvalue>noent</returnvalue>, &etc;) followed by a longer explanation."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:1338
#, no-c-format
msgid "Global commands:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: term
#: index.docbook:1341
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<command>login</command> <option>display</option> (<parameter>now</"
"parameter> | <parameter>schedule</parameter>) <parameter>user</parameter> "
"<parameter>password</parameter> [session_arguments]"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1345
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"login user at specified display. if <parameter>now</parameter> is specified, "
"a possibly running session is killed, otherwise the login is done after the "
"session exits. session_arguments are printf-like escaped contents for .dmrc. "
"Unlisted keys will default to previously saved values."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1355
#, no-c-format
msgid "Force return from console mode, even if TTY logins are still active."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: term
#: index.docbook:1361
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<command>manage</command> <parameter>display</parameter> "
"[<parameter>display_class</parameter> [<parameter>auth_name</parameter> "
"<parameter>auth_data</parameter>]]"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1365
#, no-c-format
msgid "Start managing the named foreign display."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1366
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<parameter>display_class</parameter>, if specified and non-empty, will be "
"used for configuration matching; see <xref linkend=\"kdm-files\"/>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1369
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<parameter>auth_name</parameter> and <parameter>auth_data</parameter> need "
"to be passed if the display requires X authorization. The format is the same "
"as the 2nd and 3rd column of the <command>xauth list</command> output."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: term
#: index.docbook:1377
#, no-c-format
msgid "<command>unmanage</command> <parameter>display</parameter>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1379
#, no-c-format
msgid "Stop managing the named foreign display."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:1386
#, no-c-format
msgid "Per-display commands:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1390
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The display is marked as locked. If the &X-Server; crashes in this state, no "
"auto-relogin will be performed even if the option is on."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1397
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Reverse the effect of <command>lock</command>, and re-enable auto-relogin."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1404
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The currently running session is forcibly terminated. No auto-relogin is "
"attempted, but a scheduled \"login\" command will be executed."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:1411
#, no-c-format
msgid "Commands for all sockets"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:1416
#, no-c-format
msgid "Returns a list of this socket's capabilities:"
